<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sea of Marmara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Propontis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sea of Marmara, the ancient Propontis, is an inland sea in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> through the Bosporus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> through the Dardanelles. It separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Control of its shores and straits made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, rich, and peoples such as the Bithynians lived along its southern coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara.jpg" alt="Sea of Marmara" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interiot · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_Strait_disambig.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colonists, perhaps joined by settlers from other Greek cities, occupied the triangular promontory between the Golden Horn, the Bosporus and the <b>Sea of Marmara</b>, with their acropolis on the site of the later Topkapı Palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founder, Mithridates I Ktistes (‘the Founder’), belonged to a noble family of Persian origin that had ruled the Greek city of Kios on the <b>Sea of Marmara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Milesians settled the shores of the Hellespont, the <b>Propontis</b> and the Black Sea, founding Abydos, Cyzicus, Sinope, Histria, Apollonia Pontica, Olbia and Panticapaeum, and these colonies sent grain, fish, timber and slaves in return for wine, oil and manufactured goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Odrysian rulers also hired Greek soldiers: in 400–399 BCE Seuthes II employed the survivors of Xenophon’s Ten Thousand to subdue tribes near the <b>Propontis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Samos had its own sanctuary of Hera at Naucratis in Egypt, and it founded colonies such as Perinthus on the <b>Propontis</b>, Nagidus and Celenderis in Cilicia and Minoa on Amorgos, while exiles from the island settled Dicaearchia, the later Puteoli, in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek colonies were founded along their coasts from the 8th century BCE, first on the Aegean, then on the <b>Sea of Marmara</b> and the Black Sea, and Thracians and Greeks lived side by side.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
